  "final_scientific_verdict": {
    "verdict": "FEATURE STABILITY OR SIGN DISTINCTION REGRESSED",
    "rationale": "Expressing hand landmarks strictly in a local palm-canonical frame successfully achieves mathematical in-plane rotation robustness (+8.26% advantage at -45 deg rotation over baseline). However, in Indian Sign Language, global hand orientation relative to the signer/camera is phonemic, not noise. Local palm canonicalization normalizes out the global hand orientation, causing recognition of orientation-dependent signs to regress sharply on standard upright signing: Letter H recall collapses from 100% to 41%, Letter R collapses from 100% to 67%, Letter L collapses from 74% to 49%, and overall signer_04_val accuracy drops from 100.00% to 93.34%. Consequently, pure palm canonicalization cannot replace the existing camera-space feature representation without severe performance loss."
  },
